From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Your Name <you@example.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix empty flash configuration field of the s32k148evb image

The linker script reserves FlashConfigField at 0x400 and keeps an input
section .FlashConfig for it, but no source file ever placed anything in
that section, so .FlashConfigField is zero bytes long and the image
leaves 0x400 to 0x40F untouched.

The flash controller reads that field out of reset. In erased flash it
reads 0xFF in every byte, so FSEC = 0xFF, which is SEC = 11 = secure:
the device comes up with the debug port locked and only a mass erase
brings it back. It works on a board that still carries an unsecured
field from an earlier flash run, which is why an incremental download
over an existing image does not show it, but a device programmed from
this image alone after a mass erase comes up secured.

startUp.S now fills the section with the NXP default values: backdoor
comparison key and FPROT erased, FSEC = 0xFE (unsecure, mass erase
enabled, backdoor key disabled), FOPT = 0x7F, FEPROT and FDPROT erased.
Reference: S32K1xx Series Reference Manual, Rev. 13, chapter 36.4.1
"Flash configuration field description" for the layout and 36.4.4.1.4
(FSEC) for SEC = 11 being secure.

Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.com>
---
 .../platforms/s32k148evb/main/src/bsp/startUp.S   | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/main/src/bsp/startUp.S b/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/main/src/bsp/startUp.S
index cbd41e71..c1073869 100644
--- a/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/main/src/bsp/startUp.S
+++ b/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/main/src/bsp/startUp.S
@@ -280,6 +280,21 @@ __isr_vector:
 
     .size    __isr_vector, . - __isr_vector
 
+/*
+ * Flash Configuration Field (FCF), read by the flash controller out of reset from 0x400.
+ * The linker script reserves the region, but nothing filled it, so the field stayed erased
+ * (0xFF...FF). FSEC = 0xFF means SEC = 11, that is "secure": the device comes up with the debug
+ * port locked and only a mass erase gets it back.
+ * Values below are the NXP defaults: backdoor key and FPROT erased, FSEC = 0xFE (unsecure, mass
+ * erase enabled, backdoor key disabled), FOPT = 0x7F, FEPROT = FDPROT = 0xFF.
+ */
+    .section .FlashConfig, "a"
+    .align 2
+    .long   0xFFFFFFFF      /* 0x400: backdoor comparison key, bytes 0-3 */
+    .long   0xFFFFFFFF      /* 0x404: backdoor comparison key, bytes 4-7 */
+    .long   0xFFFFFFFF      /* 0x408: FPROT3, FPROT2, FPROT1, FPROT0 */
+    .long   0xFFFF7FFE      /* 0x40C: FSEC, FOPT, FEPROT, FDPROT */
+
     .section .text // , "a" // has no effect for gcc compiler
     .equ _NVIC_ICER,  0xE000E180
     .equ _NVIC_ICPR,  0xE000E280
-- 
